src/main/java holds Pebble's main sources from Maven Central's io.pebbletemplates:pebble:4.1.1 sources
jar (pebble-4.1.1-sources.jar, SHA-1 699371e19483456ebd81e2083bb7b436d3ad412d). They are identical to
pebble/src/main/java at the v4.1.1 tag. The four classes that need optional dependencies the app does
not use are left out:

- io/pebbletemplates/pebble/loader/ServletLoader.java (javax.servlet)
- io/pebbletemplates/pebble/loader/Servlet5Loader.java (jakarta.servlet)
- io/pebbletemplates/pebble/cache/template/CaffeineTemplateCache.java (Caffeine)
- io/pebbletemplates/pebble/cache/tag/CaffeineTagCache.java (Caffeine)

Local changes (Code On The Go, ADFA-3305):

- io/pebbletemplates/pebble/lexer/LexerImpl.java matches each token against a window at the start of
  the remaining template, doubled while the regex engine reports reading its end (Matcher.hitEnd)
  and never ending inside a surrogate pair, instead of against the whole remaining template. It also
  counts the matches and windows, for a test. On Android, java.util.regex copies its whole input
  every time a Matcher is created, which made lexing quadratic in the template's length. The comment
  at LexerImpl.match says why in full.
- LexerImpl.java also keeps one Matcher per pattern, reset onto each window, and one String copy of
  each window for all the patterns tried at one position, and counts both for a test. On Android a
  new Matcher allocates native ICU state, which was most of lexing's remaining cost; each reset
  still copies the window into that Matcher's ICU buffer, which is cheap at 256 characters. The
  comment at LexerImpl.matcherOnWindow says why the reuse is safe. matcherOnWindow and the source
  and windowText fields are package-private, for a test.
- LexerImpl.java compiles the operator regex once per set of operators, shared by every lexer,
  instead of once per template; see LexerImpl.buildOperatorRegex. Its regexOperators field is
  package-private, for a test.
